{"title":"Lakeside School District","sub":"Tutoring or smaller classes?","type":"Public sector","industry":"Education","geo":"United States","diff":3,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Public sector · cost-effectiveness · stakeholder management","prompt":"Lakeside school district has 10,000 primary students; 40% are below the reading standard for their age. A $4.8M state grant must be used to raise reading results this year. Teachers' representatives want smaller classes. The superintendent has seen research on small-group tutoring. You're asked which plan would bring more students up to standard.","clarify":[{"q":"Tutoring?","a":"Three sessions a week in groups of three with trained tutors, for students below standard: about $1,200 per student a year. Local pilots: about 30% of tutored students reached the standard who otherwise wouldn't."},{"q":"Smaller classes?","a":"Cutting class sizes from 28 to 24 costs about $700 per student covered a year (more teachers and rooms). Research suggests about 6% more of the students covered reach the standard."},{"q":"Constraints?","a":"Tutors can be recruited within a term; extra classrooms aren't available in some schools."}],"structure":{"root":"Which plan brings more students up to the reading standard?","branches":[{"head":"Outcome","items":["Students reaching standard who otherwise wouldn't"]},{"head":"Cost per outcome","items":["Tutoring","Smaller classes"]},{"head":"Reach and feasibility","items":["Who each plan covers","Hiring and space"]},{"head":"Stakeholders","items":["Teachers","Parents"]}]},"hypothesis":"Tutoring targets the students who need help, so each dollar should go further. Smaller classes spread money across all students, most of whom are already at standard.","questions":[{"q":"Compare cost per extra student reaching the standard.","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p><b>Tutoring:</b> $1,200 ÷ 30% = <b>$4,000</b> per student who reaches the standard.</p> <p><b>Smaller classes:</b> $700 ÷ 6% = <b>$11,700</b>.</p> <p>Tutoring is about 2.9× as cost-effective.</p>"},{"q":"What would $4.8M achieve under each plan?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"404.66pt\" height=\"159.66pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 404.66 159.66\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 159.66 L 404.66 159.66 L 404.66 0 L 0 0 L 0 159.66 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 51.5 133.76 L 397.46 133.76 L 397.46 11.792 L 51.5 11.792 L 51.5 133.76 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"ma99844d2ee\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #4a5568; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#ma99844d2ee\" x=\"137.116364\" y=\"133.76\" style=\"fill: #4a5568; stroke: #4a5568; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #4a5568\" transform=\"translate(120.422067 149.985) scale(0.09 -0.09)\"><defs><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regular-31\" d=\"M 3520 3994 L 2099 3994 L 2099 0 L 1562 0 L 1562 3994 L 141 3994 L 141 4467 L 3520 4467 L 3520 3994 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regular-18\" d=\"M 2579 0 L 2579 538 L 2554 538 Q 2458 301 2250 112 Q 2042 -77 1626 -77 Q 1120 -77 816 252 Q 512 582 512 1184 L 512 3302 L 1024 3302 L 1024 1274 Q 1024 832 1216 608 Q 1408 384 1779 384 Q 1984 384 2166 451 Q 2349 518 2464 659 Q 2579 800 2579 1018 L 2579 3302 L 3091 3302 L 3091 0 L 2579 0 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regular-17\" d=\"M 1914 0 L 1274 0 Q 1011 0 870 153 Q 730 307 730 544 L 730 2854 L 186 2854 L 186 3302 L 493 3302 Q 659 3302 720 3369 Q 781 3437 781 3603 L 781 4218 L 1242 4218 L 1242 3302 L 1965 3302 L 1965 2854 L 1242 2854 L 1242 448 L 1914 448 L 1914 0 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regul